  "prompt_text": "The figure presents a lake restoration framework that links short-term pollution control with long-term ecological recovery and resilience. At the center is a cross-section of a lake and its watershed, showing surrounding farms, industry, a water treatment plant, forested shorelines, fish, and submerged aquatic plants. Large arrows connect three interacting themes—restoration, management, and conservation—suggesting an ongoing cycle rather than a one-time intervention.\n\nThe short-term side emphasizes reducing pollution inputs and improving environmental quality. Visible examples include intercepting agricultural runoff and industrial wastewater, removing contaminated sediments, and conserving biodiversity by introducing key species and rebalancing the food web to limit algal biomass.\n\nThe long-term side shifts toward ecosystem recovery. It highlights shoreline buffer vegetation, wetland reconstruction, and restoration of aquatic plants to improve habitat and pollutant filtering. Hydrological management is also important, with water-level regulation and reconnection to rivers and groundwater shown as ways to restore more natural water movement.\n\nA third element, community involvement, supports the whole process through sustainable fisheries and environmental education. Overall, the image conveys that lake recovery depends on combining engineering, ecological restoration, species management, and public participation across different timescales.",
